face-image: img/vista.jpg
new-page: true
on-arrival: 

= The European Question

As dreams of a scandinavian union fell through last spring, voices calling for us to join the European Economic Community (EEC) have grown confident. The recent change in French leadership have eased the Gaullist grip, opening the door for the {!<span class="tooltip-text" title="An important trading partner to our country.">United Kingdom</span>!} to join the bloc. Following London, our neighbors in Norway are hosting a referendum next year. Historical conjunctures thus seem to be aligning towards Brussels.

According to our constitution we need 149 votes in parliament in order to relinquish any sovereignity. And while such a majority would take shape, were we to vote in favour – doing so would be extremely unpopular among both the left-wing of our party as well as many privately employed workers.

How should we proceed?

- @vote-through: Europe is the future and it can't wait!
- @referendum: Our party is split, perhaps a referendum could relieve some pressure?

@vote-through
on-arrival: eu_membership += 1; left_dissent += 15; technocrat_dissent -= 10; right_worker_dissent_dissent -= 4; left_worker_dissent_dissent += 10; sf_relation -= 10; vs_relation -= 10; dkp_relation -= 10; b_relation += 6; v_relation += 6; c_relation += 6; public_homeowner_a -= 1; homeowner_a -= 4; public_prole_a -= 2; prole_a -= 7; prole_sf += 6; prole_vs += 1; prole_dkp += 1; homeowner_sf += 3; homeowner_dkp += 1; homeowner_sf += 3;
subtitle: Force the vote and join the European community!

Almost a third of our electeds abstain, some even vote against! Nonetheless, together with the bourgious parties we have secured the votes necessary.

Media pundits talk of an irrevercible break with the left, but what do they know. Incorporation will take time and requires alot of legal alignment, but in time the benefits of economic integration will surely become self-evident. [The progress towards joining the european community can be viewed on the right.]

@referendum

In order to circumvent this impasse, some of our electeds have begun airing the possibility of a referendum in order to settle the question. We have been assured by the bourgeois parties that they would accept such a proposal.

A hypothetical referendum would be held sometime in 1972, after {!<span class="tooltip-text" title="Which are to be held no later than january 1971">the upcoming folketing election</span>!}.

- @yes_referendum: A referendum is a sensible idea, let the people decide.
- @no_referendum: The time isn't right.

@yes_referendum
on-arrival: eu_referendum += 1; left_dissent += 7; technocrat_dissent -= 6; right_worker_dissent_dissent -= 2; left_worker_dissent_dissent += 3; sf_relation -= 3; vs_relation -= 3; dkp_relation -= 3; b_relation += 3; v_relation += 3; c_relation += 3;

The left already starts to bicker, as if a referendum is the most undemocratic thing they could imagine. Let us hope this is the right decision...

@no_referendum
subtitle: We may still reconsider our position at a later point...
on-arrival: technocrat_dissent += 3; b_relation -= 2;

Whether it is because we seek to inhibit membership, or because we would rather vote it through parliament. A few Pro-market voices claim we are spoiling a historic opportunity.